\section{Name Resolution Starts with Occurrences}

Consider a toy statically typed language with nested lexical scopes, no imports or overloads, and no use-before-declaration restriction. Each reference and declaration has its own identity even when spelled identically. The parser supplies an AST. A syntax-directed extractor then emits parent edges, declaration names and types, and each reference's scope, spelling, and expected type. For instance, the expected type can come from the parameter type of an already resolved built-in. Source spans stay in a host map keyed by occurrence ID. The analysis should produce a name-resolution result; it should not itself decide how to render a diagnostic or which AST node to rewrite.

Van Antwerpen and colleagues distinguish constraints on scope graph structure, reference resolution, and typing. Their framework can express interactions between binding and types and uses unification for type terms. Here we deliberately resolve names before checking type equality, with all declaration types already known. This is a small rule-based front end inspired by that separation, not an implementation of their published constraint language.

\section{Select the Nearest Declaration, Then Check Its Type}

The following is stratified, function-free Datalog with uppercase variables, lowercase constants, and safe \texttt{not} and \verb|!=| built-ins. A single synthetic scope tree contains a root declaration \(d_0\) for \texttt{x:int} and a nested declaration \(d_1\) for \texttt{x:bool}. Three references in the nested scope request an integer \texttt{x}, a boolean \texttt{x}, and an integer \texttt{y}, respectively.

\begin{verbatim}
scope(root). scope(inner). parent(inner,root).
decl(d0,root,x,int). decl(d1,inner,x,bool).
ref(r1,inner,x,int). ref(r2,inner,x,bool).
ref(r3,inner,y,int).

above(A,B) :- parent(A,B).
above(A,C) :- parent(A,B), above(B,C).
visible(A,A) :- scope(A).
visible(A,B) :- above(A,B).

cand(R,D,S,T) :- ref(R,A,N,E), visible(A,S), decl(D,S,N,T).
hidden(R,D) :- cand(R,D,S,T), cand(R,C,U,V), above(U,S).
best(R,D,T) :- cand(R,D,S,T), not hidden(R,D).
has_best(R) :- best(R,D,T).
ambiguous(R) :- best(R,D,T), best(R,C,U), D != C.
binding(R,D) :- best(R,D,T), not ambiguous(R).

unknown(R) :- ref(R,A,N,E), not has_best(R).
wrong_type(R,E,T) :- ref(R,A,N,E), binding(R,D),
                     decl(D,S,N,T), E != T.
\end{verbatim}

The positive relation \texttt{cand} is intentionally generous: it contains both declarations for each reference to \texttt{x}. Then \texttt{hidden} removes the candidate in a strict ancestor, not merely any candidate with a different scope ID. The result is \verb|binding(r1,d1)| and \verb|binding(r2,d1)|, with \verb|wrong_type(r1,int,bool)| and \verb|unknown(r3)|. No type comparison can resurrect the hidden outer declaration. In this language, changing the type needed by the context does not change lexical resolution. A language with type-directed name lookup would need a different rule system.

\Cref{fig:visual-348} makes the ambiguous-looking candidate set for \(r_1\) explicit before shadowing and type checking.

