\section{Make Shadowing and Ambiguity Regression Tests}

\begin{verbatim}
facts := extract_occurrences(ast)  # preserve IDs and spans
verify_scope_forest(facts)         # reject parent cycles first
closure := settle_ancestors_and_candidates(facts)
nearest := candidates_without_closer_competitors(closure)
for ref in source_order:
    choices := nearest[ref.id]     # diagnostics inspect settled sets
    if size(choices) != 1: diagnose_missing_or_ambiguous(ref)
    else: annotate_binding_and_compare_types(ref, only(choices))
\end{verbatim}

Delete \verb|decl(d1,inner,x,bool).| from the input. On recomputation, both \texttt{x} references bind to \(d_0\); \verb|wrong_type(r1,int,bool)| disappears and \verb|wrong_type(r2,bool,int)| appears. The missing \texttt{y} is unchanged. In a separate edit to the original input, add \verb|decl(d2,inner,x,int).|. The engine now reports \verb|ambiguous(r1)| and \verb|ambiguous(r2)|, but no binding or wrong-type fact for either reference. The front end may separately report a duplicate-declaration error at the declaration sites. It must not choose \(d_2\) just to make the integer reference pass.

The ancestor and candidate computations are monotone in accumulating facts on a fixed input; selection is not monotone across declaration insertions because a new nearer candidate can hide an old one. Even if a resolver uses negative tests to find the nearest match, those tests should only read completed inputs and lower strata. A production editor that allows incomplete files must also decide what it means to mark a scope complete. Absence in a partially extracted scope is not yet a user-facing ``unknown name.'' Test the extracted facts as well as the diagnostics: a missing parent edge and a genuinely unknown identifier can otherwise generate identical errors.

The front-end output is a mapping from occurrence IDs to declaration IDs (or a diagnostic), not an AST mutation performed during search. Keeping spans and rewrite policy outside the rules lets later compiler phases consume a settled binding result.
